// Input: nums = [1,2,3,4]
// Output: [1,3,6,10] 


class Solution {
    public int[] runningSum(int[] nums) { 

      for(int i = 1;i<nums.length;i++){

        nums[i] += nums[i-1];  

        // nums[i] = nums[i]+ nums[i-1];   ----> nums[1]= nums[1] + nums[1-1]
        //nums[1] = nums[1] +nums[0]---> nums[1] = 2 + 1 
        // nums = [1,3]

      }
      return nums;
}
}


// Explanation 

/* 

The problem asks us to find the running sum of the given array.

I use a for loop to traverse the array from index 1 because the first element is already its own running sum.

In each iteration, I add the previous running sum to the current element and store the result in the same array.

Finally, I return the updated array.

*/


// time complexity

// I use only one for loop to traverse the array once.

// Therefore, the time complexity is O(n)




// Space Complexity 

// I do not create any extra array or data structure.I update the original array.Therefore, the space complexity is O(1).



// Can it be Optimized 

// No.

// Reason:
// Every element must be visited at least once.
// No extra data structure is used.

